    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Shoulders",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Shoulders sit at 10.4% of volume with 4 sets and a flat strength index (103 to 103). Given the goal of building muscle and the fact that shoulders support both pressing and pulling patterns, they could benefit from a modest volume increase to stimulate adaptation."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Push for progressive overload on your key lifts next period: aim to add a small amount of weight or one extra rep on at least chest and leg movements, since those are closest to the 3-point threshold and are your highest-volume groups.",
    "Add one set to both biceps and shoulders next period to bring biceps up to 4 sets and shoulders up to 5 sets. Biceps at only 3 sets is likely too low for your muscle-building goal and their strength index has been completely flat.",
    "Vary your rep ranges on stalled muscle groups. For shoulders, biceps, and triceps — all showing zero index movement — try dropping into a heavier, lower-rep scheme (4 to 6 reps) for at least one set per session to introduce a new mechanical stimulus.",
    "Track how you feel during and after sessions next period. While a deload is not needed now, the stalling trend coming after a period of steady progress can sometimes reflect accumulating fatigue. If motivation or performance dips further next period, a planned deload week should be on the table."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Strength indices stalled across all muscle groups this period (best gains were chest and legs at plus 2 points each; shoulders, biceps, and triceps flat), signaling a plateau that needs a stimulus change — focus next period on progressive overload on main lifts and modest volume increases for biceps and shoulders."
}
